## Technical Note: The Effective Richardson Number

The definition of $Ri_{\text{eff}} = Ri(1 + C_\chi \chi \Delta z)$ is particularly robust because of the **similarity invariance** of $\chi \Delta z$. Even as you change your vertical grid resolution, the physical "bend" of the profile remains consistent relative to the cell size.

The use of **Gegenbauer projections** is a "pro-move" here—it ensures that the quadratic term doesn't just drift off but remains algebraically tied to the mean stability ($\zeta$). This maintains physical consistency with Monin-Obukhov scaling while still allowing for the sub-grid complexity found in datasets like SHEBA (Surface Heat Budget of the Arctic Ocean).

